From e0185ab059d0e012f6696cc47eebcb194b2a3f82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anthony Ruhier <aruhier@mailbox.org>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:22:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] clocksource/arm_arch_timer: park stopped timers

When a clockevent is shut down, the timer is disabled and its CVAL is
left at the last programmed deadline, which is in the past.

On Qualcomm X1E the secure firmware derives the APSS wake-up deadline for
cluster/system power collapse from the timer the kernel ticks on, and
reads its CVAL even when the timer is disabled. With the tick frozen in
s2idle, PSCI CPU_SUSPEND for the system state (DRIPS) then returns
success within 5 us, thousands of times per second, and the SoC never
sleeps: no DDR LPM, no CX collapse. This shows up when Linux owns EL2
(booted at EL2 via slbounce) and ticks on the physical timer itself:
CNTP under nVHE, CNTHP under VHE.

Instead of disabling the timer on shutdown, leave it enabled but masked,
with CVAL set to the largest positive value of the minimum counter width
(2^55 - 1 for 56 bits, ~59 years at 19.2 MHz). set_next_event() already
clears IT_MASK and reprograms CVAL, so re-arming is unchanged.

The firmware rejects a CVAL with bit 55 set: parking at 2^N - 1 works for
N = 50, 53 and 55, but N = 56 (the full counter width) gets no
DRIPS/CXSD/AOSD at all, as if the value were sign-extended and already
expired. Hence BIT(width - 1) - 1 rather than BIT(width) - 1.

Tested on a Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x (X1E80100), VHE: s2idle with DDR LPM,
CXSD and AOSD at 99.9% of the suspend.

Link: https://github.com/justririll/x1e-sleep/blob/main/modules/el1tmr/el1tmr.c
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/clocksource/arm_arch_timer.c | 9 ++++-----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/clocksource/arm_arch_timer.c b/drivers/clocksource/arm_arch_timer.c
index 7b4a98df6962..0fea3c5960cb 100644
--- a/drivers/clocksource/arm_arch_timer.c
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/arm_arch_timer.c
@@ -594,11 +594,10 @@ static irqreturn_t arch_timer_handler_phys(int irq, void *dev_id)
 static __always_inline int arch_timer_shutdown(const int access,
 					       struct clock_event_device *clk)
 {
-	unsigned long ctrl;
-
-	ctrl = arch_timer_reg_read_cp15(access, ARCH_TIMER_REG_CTRL);
-	ctrl &= ~ARCH_TIMER_CTRL_ENABLE;
-	arch_timer_reg_write_cp15(access, ARCH_TIMER_REG_CTRL, ctrl);
+	arch_timer_reg_write_cp15(access, ARCH_TIMER_REG_CTRL,
+				  ARCH_TIMER_CTRL_ENABLE | ARCH_TIMER_CTRL_IT_MASK);
+	arch_timer_reg_write_cp15(access, ARCH_TIMER_REG_CVAL,
+				  BIT(arch_counter_get_width() - 1) - 1);
 
 	return 0;
 }
